251
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,000
when that tenderfoot becomes an eagle scout.

252
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,000
but you really really see it happen

253
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:24,000
when you dance at your senior patrol leader's wedding,

254
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:26,000
Giving everybody a little bit of an idea

255
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:29,000
that everything is not going to happen at once.

256
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
It's very reassuring.

257
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
The youth leaders and volunteers

258
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
are pretty driven people

259
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,000
and they're very results-oriented

260
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
and they like to see immediate results for their work.

261
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
But I think we sometimes missed

262
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,000
the long game of it all.

263
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
One thing to see that right away

264
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
is where you get adult lad

265
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
or adult-driven troops.

266
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,000
It's more like yeast bread,

267
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:54,000
where you mix it up and just walk away for a while.

268
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
Artisanal scouting:

269
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:58,000
Yes,

270
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,000
The first chapter is called Troop Vision.

271
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:03,000
I like the idea of starting off with the vision

272
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:07,000
and especially working on that with the youth leaders.

273
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:10,000
There are a couple of big troops in our area,

274
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,000
but they have very different personalities.

275
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:15,000
They just really have a little bit different vision

276
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:17,000
about what we're doing.

277
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
Mark, what were you looking for in that chapter?

278
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
Just what Walter is talking about

279
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:24,000
is clarifying what your troop is about

280
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
and what it's not about.

281
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:29,000
I thought about this a lot when I was a scoutmaster:

282
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:32,000
that if you don't really have a vision and a focus,

283
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,000
you start getting pulled in all sorts of different directions,

284
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:36,000
some of which are not really beneficial

285
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:38,000
to where the troop is headed.

286
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:40,000
So it seems to me that having a troop vision

287
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:42,000
helps you clearly delineate

288
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,000
what's important and what's not important,

289
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:46,000
what you need to focus on

290
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:48,000
and what you can ignore. basically,

291
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:50,000
It's a good way to step back

292
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:52,000
Every time a troop meeting rolls around.

293
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:54,000
there's plenty of things to pay attention to.

294
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:56,000
Also, I saw the looking for strengths

295
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:59,000
and we've done something with the PLC and adults

296
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:02,000
in rating strengths and weaknesses

297
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:04,000
across the eight methods for our troop,

298
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:07,000
and that's a nice way to go through the strengths.

299
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:10,000
There's excellent advice on craft division

300
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:12,000
and sharing it, getting it into a form

301
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:16,000
that people can gather around and really work with.

302
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:19,000
Looking at the idea of evaluating your troop

303
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
Mark, you feature in this

304
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:24,000
using the Journey to Excellence program

305
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:26,000
as an evaluative tool.

306
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:28,000
That's something for too many troops.

307
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:31,000
that's a form that you fill out at reach harder time,

308
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
which is not really the point of the tool.

309
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:36,000
Obviously, it's to help you plan for excellence

310
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:38,000
and also to evaluate as you go along,

311
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:41,000
But it's just one tool in your evaluation toolbox, if you will.

312
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
The other thing that I mentioned

313
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,000
that some people are familiar with

314
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:47,000
is the Unit Commissioner Worksheet,

315
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,000
And Unit Commissioners are taught not to go in

316
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,000
and fill this out in front of the troop

317
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:54,000
because it looks like there's files from the council,

318
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:56,000
But at the same time,

319
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:59,000
if they're aware of what's on that worksheet,

320
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
that's a really valuable tool

321
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
to measure what you're doing well

322
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:05,000
and what maybe you need a little bit of help with.

323
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,000
You built another bridge

324
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:10,000
between some time disparate elements in scouting

325
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:13,000
by saying, yeah, take a look at this,

326
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,000
See what commissioners think about

327
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:18,000
and see what they're up to,

328
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:21,000
because they're not there to spy on you,

329
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:22,000
Right?

330
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:24,000
And then the third piece we've got

331
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:26,000
is this new 75 question

332
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:28,000
assessing your troop instrument,

333
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:30,000
which I think could be really valuable

334
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:32,000
for people to look at different categories

335
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:35,000
like membership, troop meetings, advancement,

336
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,000
youth leaders and so forth,

337
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:38,000
to evaluate themselves.

338
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:40,000
And I think it would be especially valuable

339
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:43,000
if a number of leaders in the troop

340
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:45,000
did this separately

341
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:47,000
and then compared their answers.

342
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,000
Have you looked at that, Walter?

343
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:50,000
Yes, I have,

344
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
And I've been recommending

345
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,000
and using the evaluation

346
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,000
in the SPL handbook.

347
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
This has a little bit more about committees

348
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,000
and things the SPL wouldn't worry about,

349
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
but it's a similar kind of thing

350
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
and a great thing to do.

351
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
And then evaluating your leadership team:

352
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
in chapter three

353
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,000
we're looking at all of the adult volunteers

354
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
in the troop, kind of helping them

355
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:15,000
sort out what their work really is

356
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:18,000
and how they fit into that overall vision.

357
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
There's also, I remember from the first volume

358
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:22,000
we talked about how we wish there was more

359
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:25,000
about the personal growth of the scout master,

360
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
which is, of course, this whole section,

361
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:28,000
Right?

362
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,000
So I think the things that we were saying

363
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
wow, I wish there was more about this

364
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
are greatly expanded in volume two.

365
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:37,000
It does answer a lot of the questions that we had

366
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:40,000
In the third chapter. you're getting the big picture.

367
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:43,000
Fourth chapter, you're kind of drilling down a little bit,

368
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,000
And then chapter five is evaluating yourself.

369
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:47,000
Yeah, chapter four is evaluating yourself.

370
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:50,000
I'm sorry, chapter four is evaluating yourself.

371
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:51,000
So why is that important?

372
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:54,000
It's sort of like a scout master conference with yourself.

373
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:58,000
Yeah, I'm looking at the five questions

374
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,000
you have listed in that chapter, Mark.

375
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
Why did you become a troop leader?

376
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,000
Why do you want to be a leader now?

377
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:06,000
What does the BSA expect of you,

378
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:07,000
What do you expect of scouting

379
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,000
and what do you expect of yourself?

380
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,000
Do people ask those questions to themselves

381
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:12,000
on a regular basis?

382
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:13,000
I'm not too sure

383
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:15,000
I don't think they do,

384
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
And especially I don't think they think about

385
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
how their motivation has changed

386
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:23,000
from the time they joined until now.

387
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:26,000
Maybe somebody experienced with his own son

388
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
who had just crossed over from cup scouting.

389
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:30,000
Well, now his son has gone off to college,

390
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:33,000
So he's in it for a different reason,

391
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:35,000
And if he doesn't think about that

392
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:37,000
then I don't know he's likely to get frustrated

393
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:40,000
or not change his approach

394
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,000
or just not do as good a job as he could.

395
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:45,000
Well, I'm definitely doing a different sort of thing now

396
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:47,000
because, as Mark mentioned,

397
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:50,000
my son's not just in college but graduated,

398
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
so I'm not doing this to spend more time with my son.

399
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
I enjoyed scouting when I was a youth.

400
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:55,000
I enjoy it now.

401
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:57,000
I enjoy working with the people here

402
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:59,000
and I should be asking myself those questions.

403
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:01,000
Those are really. it's a really interesting list,

404
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:06,000
especially, I think, as you move to a new position,

405
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:09,000
say from Scoutmaster to Assistant Scoutmaster

406
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:11,000
Or indeed the other way around.

407
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:12,000
Right,

408
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
But you know, I was Scoutmaster for three years,

409
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:18,000
then ASM, working with High Adventure,

410
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:19,000
with the older Scouts.

411
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
Those are changes where you're doing it

412
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
for a different reason or different expectations.

413
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:26,000
One of the things I frequently hear from people

414
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:29,000
who are in their second, third, fourth year

415
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:31,000
of working with a scout troop

416
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:34,000
is when they come in as your son crosses over

417
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
from Weebel O's,

418
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:39,000
You look around and there are these older Scouts.

419
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:40,000
You look at them and you go,

420
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:43,000
wow, they're really competent, knowledgeable

421
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:44,000
and everything.

422
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:47,000
And then, two or three years later,

423
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,000
your son is one of the older Scouts

424
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,000
and you wonder what happened to all those

425
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:55,000
competent, knowledgeable Scouts that were there.

426
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:56,000
You know that's what happens

427
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:59,000
And we see this when we really encourage

428
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:01,000
new parents to be on Board of Review.

429
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:02,000
Oh right,

430
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:03,000
Yeah,

431
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:05,000
Because then they'll see this 14-year-old

432
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,000
and they'll think: can Johnny?

433
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:09,000
Johnny will never be like that.

434
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:11,000
We tell him, yes, he will.

435
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:14,000
So much of the work that you do as a volunteer

436
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
in Scouting is really about perspective.

437
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:19,000
Your perspective is going to change

438
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:22,000
And if you don't keep up with your changing perspective,

439
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:23,000
you may be missing something.

440
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:24,000
That's a dialogue.

441
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
What you do, what you learn,

442
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
changes. what you know about Scouting

443
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
And I think a lot of this second volume

444
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
is the rest of the career,

445
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:33,000
Right,

446
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:34,000
Right,

447
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
If you're going to stick around for decades,

448
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,000
if not years. when you start

449
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
you're sort of like the big brother or big sister,

450
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
but then you become more of the parent figure

451
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
and then you become more of the grandparent figure.

452
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:45,000
Oh yeah,

453
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:47,000
I've definitely experienced that

454
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:50,000
When you're 60 years old and you're the Scoutmaster,

455
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:53,000
you can't really act like the big brother of the kids

456
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:55,000
and you have to find other people

457
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:57,000
to play that role for you.

458
00:20:57,000 --> 00:20:59,000
The idea of evaluating yourself in Chapter 4

459
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:01,000
is a real key, important thing,

460
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:03,000
And it's not a one-time thing, is it?

461
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:06,000
I mean, it's something that you continuously want to be doing

462
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
When you have a relationship with Scouts.

463
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
if you are anchored in who you are.

464
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,000
it's going to be easier for them.

465
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:13,000
Yes,

466
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:14,000
If you're thrashing around

467
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:17,000
and you kind of keep switching roles,

468
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,000
that's going to be a lot harder for them.

469
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:21,000
Yeah, you become kind of a moving target.

470
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:24,000
Chapter 5 is about charting the course.

471
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:27,000
What is this? charting our course?

472
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:29,000
What are we looking at there? Mark?

473
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:33,000
I see this as creating sort of a Wood Badge ticket for The Truth.

474
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:36,000
The chapter talks about creating a priority list

475
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:41,000
and then goes through the stages of team-based project planning

476
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
that are covered in Wood Badge

477
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:46,000
as a way to work that plan

478
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:49,000
so that you move toward your vision for The Truth.

479
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:52,000
This chapter reminded me of the Eagle Project workbook.

480
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:55,000
It walks you through the steps of let's do a project.

481
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:58,000
How about that? I hadn't thought of that, Walter.

482
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,000
Neither of us I like that.

483
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
Those first five chapters or so

484
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:06,000
are evaluating and planning, setting the stage,

485
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:10,000
and then a really useful chapter in Chapter 6

486
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
about how teams develop,

487
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
and anybody who's been through Wood Badge

488
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:17,000
will be familiar with the way that this chapter unfolds.

489
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:19,000
Tell us a little bit about it, Mark.

490
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:22,000
This chapter and then the next chapter on edge

491
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,000
supplement what's talked about in Wood Badge,

492
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,000
but hopefully it will add some depth-

493
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:29,000
One of the things that was fun, as I was researching

494
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:32,000
the stages of team development, was to learn

495
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:35,000
that there's actually a fifth stage, which is adjourning.

496
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:39,000
It was added to the model about 12 years after the original model

497
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:43,000
and adjourning is a stage where the team does situations like

498
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:46,000
when a high-adventure crew comes home from Fillmont

499
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:49,000
and disbands that you adjourn.

500
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:51,000
So we talked just a little bit about that.

501
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:55,000
Adjourning is an interesting thing which is often left out.

502
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:57,000
for the Fillmont crew,

503
00:22:57,000 --> 00:22:59,000
You get back together and have desserts

504
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:01,000
and watch all the pictures.

505
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:04,000
We can do a little better with those kinds of

506
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
finishing ceremonies.

507
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:07,000
We really could.

508
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:10,000
One of the things that sports has going for it

509
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,000
is that there are defined seasons.

510
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:14,000
There's a defined beginning,

511
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:16,000
There's a defined season,

512
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,000
There's a defined end.

513
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,000
There are ceremonies at the beginning and the end.

514
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,000
Scouting goes on forever

515
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
And, like you said, people leave all the time

516
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:27,000
and there's nothing that marks that occasion.

517
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:29,000
Most people will be familiar with

518
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:33,000
storming, norming, forming and performing stages.

519
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:37,000
Introducing the idea of adjourning- and what that means,

520
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:40,000
I think is another perspective that you gain over time-

521
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:44,000
is that in one way you're kind of always adjourning

522
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:48,000
That particular patrol on that particular camp out,

523
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:51,000
that crew that went on the high-adventure trip,

524
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:53,000
that patrol leaders council,

525
00:23:53,000 --> 00:23:56,000
that particular group of adult volunteers.

526
00:23:56,000 --> 00:24:00,000
Understanding adjourning happens, maybe not in major ways

527
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:04,000
but in smaller ways, in those transitional things,

528
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:08,000
kind of takes the edge off of the change part of it.

529
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:11,000
that can be a little difficult sometimes.

530
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:14,000
There are these small adjournings you can do like

531
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:17,000
thorns and roses at the end of even an overnight.

532
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:21,000
But there is this: you can't build the same camp for it twice,

533
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:23,000
or whatever it was. Heraclitus said:

534
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:24,000
Oh, that's a good thought.

535
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:26,000
This steps way ahead in the book.

536
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:30,000
but in chapter 17,, which is about older scouts,

537
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:34,000
a scouter named Steve Myers gave me some great stuff

538
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:37,000
about the concept of rites of passage,

539
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:41,000
Intentionally building those in Scatmaster Conferences

540
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:44,000
in other ways, so that you mark a young man's growth,

541
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:46,000
And I think that relates back to talking about

542
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:49,000
how there's no off-ramp, so to speak.

543
00:24:49,000 --> 00:24:51,000
Those rites of passage give you that off-ramp.

544
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:54,000
So maybe you do have some sort of formal ceremony

545
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:58,000
when that 18-year-old stops being a scout

546
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:00,000
and starts being an assistant scoutmaster

547
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:03,000
and you formally present him with an assistant scoutmaster patch

