00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:02,000
That actually came out at one of my Fillmont conferences,

596
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:04,000
Building Stronger troops a couple of years ago.

597
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:08,000
And if you think about that situation where a scout is struggling

598
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:11,000
to get a fire going, for example,

599
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:14,000
if you go up and you have your hands in your pockets,

600
00:27:14,000 --> 00:27:16,000
you're not going to take over for him.

601
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:19,000
You're going to let him struggle a little bit with that and learn.

602
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:22,000
Mark, this is exactly my example that I've told Clark before.

603
00:27:22,000 --> 00:27:26,000
If you want to be a scout leader, ask yourself

604
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:29,000
how long can you stand there with your hands in your pockets

605
00:27:29,000 --> 00:27:32,000
watching a scout try to build a fire?

606
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:35,000
And the number should be something over 35 minutes,

607
00:27:35,000 --> 00:27:39,000
unless there's a hypothermia situation.

608
00:27:39,000 --> 00:27:40,000
Right,

609
00:27:40,000 --> 00:27:44,000
What I really like about this is there are very practical,

